nyaterm (Telnet session backend, native/zssh-protocols/)
  https://github.com/nyakang/nyaterm
  Copyright 2026 Kang and contributors
  License: Apache-2.0

  Adapted from nyaterm's crates/nyaterm-transport: telnet_codec.rs (Telnet
  command/option constants, NAWS frame layout, IAC escaping of application
  data), telnet_input.rs (Enter normalization, local-echo rules and the
  escape-sequence tracker) and telnet_prompts.rs plus the auto-login state in
  lib.rs (control-sequence stripping, prompt tail window, "Last login" guard,
  failure phrases). The files carry a header comment naming their source.
  The IAC parser was rewritten as a state machine that keeps its state across
  reads, option negotiation was extended (TTYPE, BINARY, per-option state),
  and the local line editor was not ported; everything else was changed to fit
  ZeeTerm's session abstractions. Apache-2.0 is compatible with distribution
  under GPL-3.0-or-later. Only nyaterm's design and code under Apache-2.0 were
  used; nothing was taken from projects under GPL-3.0-only.

Telnet and serial backend dependencies (native/zssh-protocols/)
  encoding_rs 0.8 (character set conversion for GBK/Big5/Shift-JIS/EUC-KR/
  Latin1) - (Apache-2.0 OR MIT) AND BSD-3-Clause; zeroize 1 (clears the
  auto-login password after use) - Apache-2.0 OR MIT; windows 0.62 (Win32
  serial API and SetupAPI port listing) - MIT OR Apache-2.0. All three were
  already part of the resolved tree of native/Cargo.lock and are used
  unmodified from crates.io. The serial backend calls the Win32 API directly;
  the serialport crate (MPL-2.0) is deliberately not used.

nyaterm (X11 forwarding)
  https://github.com/nyakang/nyaterm
  Copyright 2026 Kang and contributors
  License: Apache-2.0 (compatible with distribution under GPL-3.0-or-later)

  The X11 connection-setup packet handling in
  crates/zssh-core/src/x11/cookie.rs (packet length, byte-order aware field
  reads, MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE replacement, hex helpers) was adapted from
  crates/nyaterm-transport/src/x11.rs (X11AuthRewriter, setup_packet_len,
  rewrite_x11_auth_setup_packet). Changes: the fake cookie is looked up in a
  per-connection distribution table before it is replaced, parsing is
  bounds-checked, and the fake cookie comes from the OS random source. The
  file keeps an "Adapted from nyaterm" header comment. The per-connection
  routing, limits and failure handling are written independently.

VcXsrv (optional local X server, not distributed with ZeeTerm)
  https://github.com/marchaesen/vcxsrv
  License: GPL-3.0 (VcXsrv's own code; the bundled X.Org components are under
  MIT/X11-style licenses)

  ZeeTerm does not ship any VcXsrv file. If the user asks for it, ZeeTerm
  downloads the official installer (the release pinned in
  crates/zssh-core/src/x11/local/install.rs) from the project's
  GitHub release page onto the user's machine, verifies its SHA-256, and runs
  it with the user's UAC confirmation. VcXsrv is then started as a separate
  program.
